CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ if\(int x=3\): branch taken, x==3: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ if\(int y=0\): else taken, y==0: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ if\(int \*p=&storage\): p non-null, \*p==42: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ if\(int z=make\(5\)\): z==5: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.\d+\] line \d+ condition initializer runs once: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^.*unreachable: &storage is never null.*: FAILURE$
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
C++ [stmt.select]/1-2, [stmt.if]: an if-statement condition may be a
declaration; the value tested is the declared variable contextually converted
to bool.  The C++ frontend (cpp_typecheck_code.cpp typecheck_ifthenelse) used
to type-check the declaration but leave the condition as the declaration code,
so the "then" branch was never taken.  The fix rewrites `if(T v = e) S1 [else
S2]` into `{ T v = e; if(v) S1 [else S2] }`, mirroring the existing handling in
typecheck_while / typecheck_switch.  This also fixed libstdc++
vector::_M_erase_at_end's `if (size_type __n = _M_finish - __pos)` (used by
clear()/resize()/erase(first, last)), which had silently been a no-op.
